"use strict";(self.webpackChunkucl_artificial_intelligence_society=self.webpackChunkucl_artificial_intelligence_society||[]).push([[5035],{1660:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"collection":"tutorials","route":"/learn/tutorials/machine-learning/session-1-intro-to-machine-learning.md/","sourcePath":"knowledge/tutorials/machine-learning/session-1-intro-to-machine-learning.md","fileUrl":"https://github.com/UCLAIS/UCLAIS.github.io/blob/14dac60a21d5e860ecbb5ecdeb260c19147e5676/knowledge/tutorials/machine-learning/session-1-intro-to-machine-learning.md","format":"md","document":{"id":"knowledge:tutorials/machine-learning/session-1-intro-to-machine-learning.md","title":"Intro to Machine Learning","sections":[{"id":"section-1","heading":"Intro to Machine Learning","text":"Session 1 of 7","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Session 1 of 7"}]}]},{"id":"section-2","heading":"What is Machine Learning? (slide 2)","text":"**Key idea:** Instead of writing rules, show examples and let the model find the rules.\\n\\n- **Data + Model:** A systematic way to measure and improve predictions.\\n- **Data-Driven Logic:** Programs learn patterns from examples instead of explicit code.\\n- **No Hand-Written Rules:** Complex problems evade rigid if-else programming structures.\\n- **Classic Application:** Email spam filters trained on thousands of labelled messages.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key idea:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Instead of writing rules, show examples and let the model find the rules."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data + Model:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" A systematic way to measure and improve predictions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data-Driven Logic:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Programs learn patterns from examples instead of explicit code."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No Hand-Written Rules:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Complex problems evade rigid if-else programming structures."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classic Application:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Email spam filters trained on thousands of labelled messages."}]}]}]}]},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Types of Learning (slides 3\u20135)","text":"- **Supervised:** Predict target outcomes using labelled historical examples.\\n- **Unsupervised:** Discover hidden structures in data without explicit labels.\\n- **Reinforcement:** Learn optimal actions via trial, error, and reward signals.\\n\\nMost of this tutorial series focuses on supervised learning: predicting y from x.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supervised:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Predict target outcomes using labelled historical examples."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unsupervised:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Discover hidden structures in data without explicit labels."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reinforcement:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Learn optimal actions via trial, error, and reward signals."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most of this tutorial series focuses on supervised learning: predicting y from x."}]}]},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Supervised Learning (slide 6)","text":"- **Data:** Collect and clean raw information.\\n- **Features:** Choose inputs (x) and labels (y).\\n- **Model:** Pick architecture and loss function.\\n- **Train:** Evaluate parameters on unseen data.\\n- **Test:** Compare results against test data, diagnose overfitting\\n\\n**Correction:** The \u201cTrain\u201d caption should refer to fitting training data. Unseen data belongs to testing, as slide 7 explains.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Collect and clean raw information."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Choose inputs (x) and labels (y)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Pick architecture and loss function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Train:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Evaluate parameters on unseen data."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Compare results against test data, diagnose overfitting"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correction:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The \u201cTrain\u201d caption should refer to fitting training data. Unseen data belongs to testing, as slide 7 explains."}]}]},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Generalisation (slide 7)","text":"**Train Split**\\n\\nFit the parameters.\\n\\nModel learns patterns directly from this specific dataset.\\n\\n**Test Split**\\n\\nJudge the performance.\\n\\nUnseen data used strictly to verify real-world accuracy.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Train Split"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fit the parameters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model learns patterns directly from this specific dataset."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test Split"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Judge the performance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unseen data used strictly to verify real-world accuracy."}]}]},{"id":"section-6","heading":"Linear Regression (slides 8\u201312)","text":"**Basic Idea:** How can a machine create a line of best fit for any given graph?","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Idea:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" How can a machine create a line of best fit for any given graph?"}]}]},{"id":"section-7","heading":"Predict a Number (slide 13)","text":"Output is continuous (e.g. house price).\\n\\n**One feature:** `\u0177 = wx + b`\\n\\n**Weights and Intercept:** Learn the slope (w) and baseline (b).\\n\\n**Multiple Features:** Expand to many input variables.\\n\\n**Many features:** `\u0177 = w\u2081x\u2081 + w\u2082x\u2082 + \u2026 + w_d x_d + b = w\u1d40x + b`","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output is continuous (e.g. house price)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One feature:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u0177 = wx + b"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Weights and Intercept:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Learn the slope (w) and baseline (b)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple Features:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Expand to many input variables."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many features:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u0177 = w\u2081x\u2081 + w\u2082x\u2082 + \u2026 + w_d x_d + b = w\u1d40x + b"}]}]},{"id":"section-8","heading":"Least Squares (slide 14)","text":"**Residuals:** The difference between the actual value and the prediction.\\n\\n`e\u1d62 = y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62`\\n\\n**Squared Error:** Squaring stops negative/positive signs from cancelling out and amplifies large error.\\n\\n`L(w,b) = (1 / (2n)) \u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)\xb2`","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Residuals:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The difference between the actual value and the prediction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e\u1d62 = y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Squared Error:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Squaring stops negative/positive signs from cancelling out and amplifies large error."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"L(w,b) = (1 / (2n)) \u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)\xb2"}]}]},{"id":"section-9","heading":"Solving It (slides 15\u201318)","text":"**Method 1: Finding the minimum directly**\\n\\n**Normal Equations**\\n\\nExact closed-form mathematical solution in a single step.\\n\\nGets very slow with millions of features.\\n\\n`w = (X\u1d40X)\u207b\xb9X\u1d40y`\\n\\n**Method 2: Using Gradient Descent**\\n\\n**Key idea:** The loss function is a bowl. Gradient descent rolls to the bottom.\\n\\nRepeatedly step downhill opposite to the gradient.\\n\\nScales efficiently to massive datasets.\\n\\n`w \u2190 w \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202w`, `b \u2190 b \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202b`\\n\\nFrom [GeeksforGeeks](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/machine-learning/what-is-gradient-descent/) and [Data Science Wizards](https://medium.com/@datasciencewizards/a-simple-guide-to-gradient-descent-algorithm-60cbb66a0df9) (slide 17).\\n\\n**Note:** The inverse requires `X\u1d40X` to be invertible. Slide 18\'s non-convex illustration is not the shape of this linear least-squares loss.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method 1: Finding the minimum directly"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normal Equations"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact closed-form mathematical solution in a single step."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gets very slow with millions of features."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w = (X\u1d40X)\u207b\xb9X\u1d40y"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method 2: Using Gradient Descent"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key idea:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The loss function is a bowl. Gradient descent rolls to the bottom."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repeatedly step downhill opposite to the gradient."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales efficiently to massive datasets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w \u2190 w \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202w"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b \u2190 b \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202b"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/machine-learning/what-is-gradient-descent/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"GeeksforGeeks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"https://medium.com/@datasciencewizards/a-simple-guide-to-gradient-descent-algorithm-60cbb66a0df9","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data Science Wizards"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (slide 17)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The inverse requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X\u1d40X"},{"type":"text","value":" to be invertible. Slide 18\'s non-convex illustration is not the shape of this linear least-squares loss."}]}]},{"id":"section-10","heading":"Where do we stop? (slide 19)","text":"**Gradient Descent**\\n\\nRepeatedly step downhill opposite to the gradient.\\n\\nScales efficiently to massive datasets.\\n\\nWhere do we stop?","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gradient Descent"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repeatedly step downhill opposite to the gradient."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales efficiently to massive datasets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where do we stop?"}]}]},{"id":"section-11","heading":"Session 1: Linear Regression - Hands-on Exercises (notebook cell 1)","text":"[Answers notebook](https://uclai.co.uk/materials/tutorials/machine-learning/tutorial-1-linear-regression-answers.ipynb)","kind":"exercise","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://uclai.co.uk/materials/tutorials/machine-learning/tutorial-1-linear-regression-answers.ipynb","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Answers notebook"}]}]}]},{"id":"section-12","heading":"Notebook setup (notebook cell 2)","text":"```python\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\nrng = np.random.default_rng(22)\\nplt.rcParams.update({\\"figure.figsize\\": (6, 4), \\"axes.grid\\": True, \\"grid.alpha\\": 0.3})\\n```","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"import numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\nrng = np.random.default_rng(22)\\nplt.rcParams.update({\\"figure.figsize\\": (6, 4), \\"axes.grid\\": True, \\"grid.alpha\\": 0.3})","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-13","heading":"1. Generate data and split it (notebook cell 3)","text":"We create data from a known rule, `y = 2x + 1 + noise`, so we can check whether the model recovers it.\\nThen we split into a **train** set (fit the parameters) and a **test** set (judge the performance).","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We create data from a known rule, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y = 2x + 1 + noise"},{"type":"text","value":", so we can check whether the model recovers it.\\nThen we split into a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"train"}]},{"type":"text","value":" set (fit the parameters) and a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"test"}]},{"type":"text","value":" set (judge the performance)."}]}]},{"id":"section-14","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 4)","text":"```python\\nn = 50\\nw_true, b_true = 2.0, 1.0\\n# choosing random x values between 0, 10\\nx = rng.uniform(0, 10, n)\\nprint(x)\\n# making the line\\ny = w_true * x + b_true + rng.normal(0, 1.5, n) # adding some noise (gotta make life annoying for ourselves)\\n# using the first 40 values as training, last 40-50 as the testing data \\nidx = rng.permutation(n)\\ntrain, test = idx[:40], idx[40:]\\nx_tr, y_tr = x[train], y[train]\\nx_te, y_te = x[test],  y[test]\\n\\n#plotting it\\nplt.scatter(x_tr, y_tr, label=\\"train\\")\\nplt.scatter(x_te, y_te, color=\\"orange\\", label=\\"test\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"Feature (x)\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"Target (y)\\"); plt.legend(); plt.show()\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"n = 50\\nw_true, b_true = 2.0, 1.0\\n# choosing random x values between 0, 10\\nx = rng.uniform(0, 10, n)\\nprint(x)\\n# making the line\\ny = w_true * x + b_true + rng.normal(0, 1.5, n) # adding some noise (gotta make life annoying for ourselves)\\n# using the first 40 values as training, last 40-50 as the testing data \\nidx = rng.permutation(n)\\ntrain, test = idx[:40], idx[40:]\\nx_tr, y_tr = x[train], y[train]\\nx_te, y_te = x[test],  y[test]\\n\\n#plotting it\\nplt.scatter(x_tr, y_tr, label=\\"train\\")\\nplt.scatter(x_te, y_te, color=\\"orange\\", label=\\"test\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"Feature (x)\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"Target (y)\\"); plt.legend(); plt.show()","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-15","heading":"2. Predict a number: guess a line (notebook cell 5)","text":"The model is `\u0177 = wx + b`, where `w` is the slope and `b` is the baseline (intercept).\\n\\n**Exercise 2.** Change `w` and `b` below until the line looks like a good fit by eye.\\nTry the flat line `w = 0, b = 10` from the slides first.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u0177 = wx + b"},{"type":"text","value":", where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w"},{"type":"text","value":" is the slope and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b"},{"type":"text","value":" is the baseline (intercept)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exercise 2."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Change "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b"},{"type":"text","value":" below until the line looks like a good fit by eye.\\nTry the flat line "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w = 0, b = 10"},{"type":"text","value":" from the slides first."}]}]},{"id":"section-16","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 6)","text":"```python\\ndef predict(x, w, b):\\n    return w*x + b\\n\\nw, b = 10, 10 # try change these around and find the best parameters by hand\\n\\nxs = np.linspace(0, 10, 100)\\nplt.scatter(x_tr, y_tr)\\nplt.plot(xs, predict(xs, w, b), \\"r\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"Feature (x)\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"Target (y)\\"); plt.title(f\\"w={w}, b={b}\\"); plt.show()\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"def predict(x, w, b):\\n    return w*x + b\\n\\nw, b = 10, 10 # try change these around and find the best parameters by hand\\n\\nxs = np.linspace(0, 10, 100)\\nplt.scatter(x_tr, y_tr)\\nplt.plot(xs, predict(xs, w, b), \\"r\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"Feature (x)\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"Target (y)\\"); plt.title(f\\"w={w}, b={b}\\"); plt.show()","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-17","heading":"3. Least squares loss (notebook cell 7)","text":"The **residual** is the actual value minus the prediction: `e\u1d62 = y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62`.\\nSquaring stops positive and negative errors cancelling, and we average them:\\n\\n`L(w,b) = (1 / (2n)) \u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)\xb2`","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"residual"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the actual value minus the prediction: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e\u1d62 = y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62"},{"type":"text","value":".\\nSquaring stops positive and negative errors cancelling, and we average them:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"L(w,b) = (1 / (2n)) \u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)\xb2"}]}]},{"id":"section-18","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 8)","text":"```python\\ndef loss(w, b, x, y):\\n    residuals = y - predict(x,w,b)\\n    return np.mean(residuals ** 2) / 2\\n\\nfor (w_, b_) in [(0, 10), (1, 0), (2, 1), (3, 5)]:\\n    print(f\\"w={w_}, b={b_}:  loss = {loss(w_, b_, x_tr, y_tr):.2f}\\")\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"def loss(w, b, x, y):\\n    residuals = y - predict(x,w,b)\\n    return np.mean(residuals ** 2) / 2\\n\\nfor (w_, b_) in [(0, 10), (1, 0), (2, 1), (3, 5)]:\\n    print(f\\"w={w_}, b={b_}:  loss = {loss(w_, b_, x_tr, y_tr):.2f}\\")","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-19","heading":"Exercise 3 (notebook cell 9)","text":"Which guess has the lowest loss? Does it match what you saw by eye in Section 2?\\n\\nNow look at the loss for *every* pair `(w,b)`. It forms a **bowl**, and the lowest point is the best line.","kind":"exercise","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which guess has the lowest loss? Does it match what you saw by eye in Section 2?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now look at the loss for "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every"}]},{"type":"text","value":" pair "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(w,b)"},{"type":"text","value":". It forms a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"bowl"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and the lowest point is the best line."}]}]},{"id":"section-20","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 10)","text":"```python\\nW, B = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(-1, 5, 200), np.linspace(-5, 8, 200))\\nZ = np.array([[loss(wi, bi, x_tr, y_tr) for wi in W[0]] for bi in B[:, 0]])\\n\\nplt.contourf(W, B, Z, levels=30, cmap=\\"viridis\\")\\nplt.colorbar(label=\\"Loss\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"w\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"b\\"); plt.title(\\"The loss is a bowl\\"); plt.show()\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"W, B = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(-1, 5, 200), np.linspace(-5, 8, 200))\\nZ = np.array([[loss(wi, bi, x_tr, y_tr) for wi in W[0]] for bi in B[:, 0]])\\n\\nplt.contourf(W, B, Z, levels=30, cmap=\\"viridis\\")\\nplt.colorbar(label=\\"Loss\\")\\nplt.xlabel(\\"w\\"); plt.ylabel(\\"b\\"); plt.title(\\"The loss is a bowl\\"); plt.show()","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-21","heading":"4. Method 1: find the minimum directly (normal equations) (notebook cell 11)","text":"Set the gradient to zero. In matrix form, with `X` = a column of `x` values plus a column of ones:\\n\\n`\u2207w L = 0 \u21d2 w = (X\u1d40X)\u207b\xb9X\u1d40y`\\n\\nHere `w` holds both the slope and the intercept.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set the gradient to zero. In matrix form, with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X"},{"type":"text","value":" = a column of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" values plus a column of ones:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u2207w L = 0 \u21d2 w = (X\u1d40X)\u207b\xb9X\u1d40y"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w"},{"type":"text","value":" holds both the slope and the intercept."}]}]},{"id":"section-22","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 12)","text":"```python\\nX_tr = np.column_stack([x_tr, np.ones_like(x_tr)])\\n# linalg.solve solves for w in the form aw = b\\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X_tr.T @ X_tr, X_tr.T @ y_tr)\\nw_ne, b_ne = theta\\nprint(f\\"Normal equations:  w = {w_ne:.4f}, b = {b_ne:.4f}   (truth: w=2, b=1)\\")\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"X_tr = np.column_stack([x_tr, np.ones_like(x_tr)])\\n# linalg.solve solves for w in the form aw = b\\ntheta = np.linalg.solve(X_tr.T @ X_tr, X_tr.T @ y_tr)\\nw_ne, b_ne = theta\\nprint(f\\"Normal equations:  w = {w_ne:.4f}, b = {b_ne:.4f}   (truth: w=2, b=1)\\")","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-23","heading":"Exercise 4 (notebook cell 13)","text":"Re-run Sections 1 and 4 with `n = 10`, then `n = 500`. How close do `w` and `b` get to the truth, and why?","kind":"exercise","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Re-run Sections 1 and 4 with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"n = 10"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"n = 500"},{"type":"text","value":". How close do "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b"},{"type":"text","value":" get to the truth, and why?"}]}]},{"id":"section-24","heading":"5. Method 2: gradient descent (notebook cell 14)","text":"Instead of solving in one step, repeatedly step downhill:\\n\\n`w \u2190 w \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202w`, `b \u2190 b \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202b`\\n\\nwith\\n\\n`\u2202L/\u2202w = \u2212(2/n) \u03a3 x\u1d62(y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)`, `\u2202L/\u2202b = \u2212(2/n) \u03a3 (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)`\\n\\n`\u03b7` (eta) is the **learning rate**. We stop when the loss barely changes (`tol`) or after `max_iter` steps.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instead of solving in one step, repeatedly step downhill:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"w \u2190 w \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202w"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b \u2190 b \u2212 \u03b7 \u2202L/\u2202b"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"with"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u2202L/\u2202w = \u2212(2/n) \u03a3 x\u1d62(y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u2202L/\u2202b = \u2212(2/n) \u03a3 (y\u1d62 \u2212 \u0177\u1d62)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u03b7"},{"type":"text","value":" (eta) is the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"learning rate"}]},{"type":"text","value":". We stop when the loss barely changes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tol"},{"type":"text","value":") or after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"max_iter"},{"type":"text","value":" steps."}]}]},{"id":"section-25","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 15)","text":"```python\\ndef gradient_descent(x, y, eta=0.01, max_iter=5000, tol=1e-9, w0=0.0, b0=0.0):\\n    w, b = w0, b0\\n    n = len(x)\\n    history = [(w, b, loss(w, b, x, y))]\\n    for k in range(max_iter):\\n        e = y - predict(x, w, b)# residuals\\n        dw = -(2 / n) * np.sum(x * e)\\n        db = -(2 / n) * np.sum(e)\\n        w, b = w - eta * dw, b - eta * db      # step downhill\\n        history.append((w, b, loss(w, b, x, y)))\\n        if abs(history[-2][2] - history[-1][2]) < tol:   # cutoff: loss stopped improving\\n            break\\n    return w, b, np.array(history)\\n\\nw_gd, b_gd, hist = gradient_descent(x_tr, y_tr, eta=0.01)\\nprint(f\\"Stopped after {len(hist)-1} steps\\")\\nprint(f\\"Gradient descent:  w = {w_gd:.4f}, b = {b_gd:.4f}\\")\\nprint(f\\"Normal equations:  w = {w_ne:.4f}, b = {b_ne:.4f}\\")\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"def gradient_descent(x, y, eta=0.01, max_iter=5000, tol=1e-9, w0=0.0, b0=0.0):\\n    w, b = w0, b0\\n    n = len(x)\\n    history = [(w, b, loss(w, b, x, y))]\\n    for k in range(max_iter):\\n        e = y - predict(x, w, b)# residuals\\n        dw = -(2 / n) * np.sum(x * e)\\n        db = -(2 / n) * np.sum(e)\\n        w, b = w - eta * dw, b - eta * db      # step downhill\\n        history.append((w, b, loss(w, b, x, y)))\\n        if abs(history[-2][2] - history[-1][2]) < tol:   # cutoff: loss stopped improving\\n            break\\n    return w, b, np.array(history)\\n\\nw_gd, b_gd, hist = gradient_descent(x_tr, y_tr, eta=0.01)\\nprint(f\\"Stopped after {len(hist)-1} steps\\")\\nprint(f\\"Gradient descent:  w = {w_gd:.4f}, b = {b_gd:.4f}\\")\\nprint(f\\"Normal equations:  w = {w_ne:.4f}, b = {b_ne:.4f}\\")","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-26","heading":"Exercise 5 (notebook cell 16)","text":"Change `tol` to `1e-3`, then `1e-12`. How many steps does it take each time, and how far from the normal-equation answer do you end up? This is the trade-off in choosing a cutoff: a loose cutoff stops early with a mostly right answer.","kind":"exercise","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tol"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1e-3"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1e-12"},{"type":"text","value":". How many steps does it take each time, and how far from the normal-equation answer do you end up? This is the trade-off in choosing a cutoff: a loose cutoff stops early with a mostly right answer."}]}]},{"id":"section-27","heading":"6. The learning rate (notebook cell 17)","text":"Too small and it crawls; too large and it overshoots. Compare four values of `\u03b7`.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Too small and it crawls; too large and it overshoots. Compare four values of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\u03b7"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"id":"section-28","heading":"Solution code (notebook cell 18)","text":"```python\\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\\nfor eta in [0.001, 0.01, 0.025, 0.035]:\\n    _, _, h = gradient_descent(x_tr, y_tr, eta=eta, max_iter=100, tol=0)\\n    ax.plot(h[:, 2], label=f\\"eta = {eta}\\")\\nax.set_yscale(\\"log\\"); ax.set_xlabel(\\"Iteration\\"); ax.set_ylabel(\\"Loss (log scale)\\")\\nax.legend(); plt.show()\\n```","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"code","value":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\\nfor eta in [0.001, 0.01, 0.025, 0.035]:\\n    _, _, h = gradient_descent(x_tr, y_tr, eta=eta, max_iter=100, tol=0)\\n    ax.plot(h[:, 2], label=f\\"eta = {eta}\\")\\nax.set_yscale(\\"log\\"); ax.set_xlabel(\\"Iteration\\"); ax.set_ylabel(\\"Loss (log scale)\\")\\nax.legend(); plt.show()","language":"python"}]},{"id":"section-29","heading":"Exercise 6 (notebook cell 19)","text":"Which values converge (work)?","kind":"exercise","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which values converge (work)?"}]}]},{"id":"section-30","heading":"Solution to Exercise 6 (notebook cell 19)","text":"all but red (they settle at a low value whereas red increases with iterations)","kind":"solution","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"all but red (they settle at a low value whereas red increases with iterations)"}]}]},{"id":"section-31","heading":"Publication notes","text":"**Notebook correction:** Section 5 uses twice the gradient of the stated half mean squared error. The matching gradients are `dw = -np.mean(x * e)` and `db = -np.mean(e)`, where `e = y - predict(x, w, b)`. Original code and equations are preserved.\\n\\nSaved notebook outputs are omitted, including the plot colours referenced in Exercise 6. Slide diagrams are not reproduced here; slide and notebook references identify the source material.\\n\\nFor unanswered questions or corrections, email [info@uclai.co.uk](mailto:info@uclai.co.uk). AI answers may be incorrect; check the sources.","kind":"explanation","nodes":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Notebook correction:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Section 5 uses twice the gradient of the stated half mean squared error. The matching gradients are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dw = -np.mean(x * e)"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"db = -np.mean(e)"},{"type":"text","value":", where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e = y - predict(x, w, b)"},{"type":"text","value":". Original code and equations are preserved."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Saved notebook outputs are omitted, including the plot colours referenced in Exercise 6. Slide diagrams are not reproduced here; slide and notebook references identify the source material."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For unanswered questions or corrections, email "},{"type":"link","href":"mailto:info@uclai.co.uk","children":[{"type":"text","value":"info@uclai.co.uk"}]},{"type":"text","value":". AI answers may be incorrect; check the sources."}]}]}],"type":"tutorial","topics":["machine-learning","supervised-learning","linear-regression","least-squares","gradient-descent","numpy"],"series":"Machine Learning Tutorial Series","order":1,"archived":true,"origin":"curated","reviewedAt":"2026-10-30T00:23:50.000Z","source":{"url":"https://github.com/UCLAIS/UCLAIS.github.io/blob/14dac60a21d5e860ecbb5ecdeb260c19147e5676/knowledge/tutorials/machine-learning/session-1-intro-to-machine-learning.md","revision":"14dac60a21d5e860ecbb5ecdeb260c19147e5676","attribution":"UCL AI Society","status":"approved"}},"releaseId":"787fa0368a8165143718a42ecca931753b5d583af419db0496a45f146dae38fe"}')}}]);